Define the comparative similarity language CSL as follows. Fix a countable set of propositional variables. Formulas are built from variables using the Boolean connectives and a binary operator "closer(·,·)" applied to formulas. A distance model is a triple M = (W, d, V), where W is a non-empty set, d : W × W → [0,∞) is a distance function (possibly required to satisfy additional axioms such as d(x,x)=0, symmetry, the triangle inequality, separation, or to be a full metric), and V assigns to each propositional variable a subset of W. The extension ⟦τ⟧ of a formula τ is defined inductively, with Boolean cases standard, and

  M, x ⊨ closer(τ₁, τ₂)  iff  inf{ d(x,y) : y ∈ ⟦τ₁⟧ } < inf{ d(x,y) : y ∈ ⟦τ₂⟧ },

with the infimum of the empty set taken to be +∞. A CSL-formula φ is valid in a class K of distance models if M, x ⊨ φ for every M ∈ K and every x in the domain of M.

Problem: For each of the following classes K, provide a sound and complete finite (or recursive) axiomatisation of the set of CSL-formulas valid in K:
(a) all distance models;
(b) all symmetric distance models;
(c) all distance models satisfying the triangle inequality;
(d) all metric models;
(e) all metric models induced by ℝⁿ with the Euclidean metric (n ≥ 1).

